H("Variance structure (RQ1)", 2)
P(f"Between-journal differences explained {pc(vc['sigma2_journal']/tot)} of the variance of ln D (95% CI {pc(vci['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(vci['icc_journal'][1])}), journal × year differences {pc(vc['sigma2_year']/tot)} and residual between-article variation {pc(vc['sigma2_resid']/tot)}. Leaving out one publisher at a time gave journal shares of {pc(min(C['x456']['X4b_ICC_leave_one_publisher_out'].values()))}–{pc(max(C['x456']['X4b_ICC_leave_one_publisher_out'].values()))}; excluding 75 flagged journals gave {pc(C['x456']['X5_ICC_excl_flagged'])}; the article-type variants gave {pc(min(v['icc_journal'] for k, v in Y3.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and 'shortfall' in v))}–{pc(max(v['icc_journal'] for k, v in Y3.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and 'shortfall' in v))}. {M['vc_ext']['n_journals']} non-frame PubMed journals gave {pc(M['vc_ext']['estimate']['icc_journal'])} (wide CI {pc(M['vc_ext']['ci95']['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(M['vc_ext']['ci95']['icc_journal'][1])}).")
H("Forecast accuracy (RQ2)", 2)
P(f"The evaluation covered {M['n_journal_origins']:,} journal-origins from {M['n_journals_forecast']} journals and {M['n_target_papers']:,} target papers. Journal-specific forecasts clearly beat the publisher-pooled forecast (F0 − F2 pinball difference {y0c['F0-F2']['est']:+.4f}; imprint-cluster bootstrap CI {y0c['F0-F2']['lo']:+.4f} to {y0c['F0-F2']['hi']:+.4f}; skill {pc(fm['F0']['skill'])}; same sign in {y0c['F0-F2']['sign_consistent']}/{y0c['F0-F2']['n_imprints']} imprints; imprint-mean difference {Z3['F0-F2']['mean_of_imprint_means']:+.4f}, t(6) CI {Z3['F0-F2']['t6_ci'][0]:+.4f} to {Z3['F0-F2']['t6_ci'][1]:+.4f}). Differences among the journal-specific forecasters were below 2% of loss (Tables 1 and 2). With t-based imprint intervals (6 degrees of freedom) and Holm adjustment over seven contrasts (Table 2), only one contrast survived Holm adjustment and a publisher-level check: the value of journal information (F0 − F2). The coverage shortfall (below) was negative in all three publishers (unadjusted publisher-level p = 0.024, not part of the Holm family). F0 had lower loss than the lognormal F3 and empirical-Bayes F4 in all publishers (journal-mean differences {con['F3 - F0']['mean_diff']:+.4f} and {con['F4 - F0']['mean_diff']:+.4f}), but the Holm-adjusted imprint-level p was {Z3['F4-F0']['p_t_holm']:.3f} and the publisher-level p about {Z3['F4-F0']['publisher_level_p_t2']:.1f}; F6 was marginally better than F0 ({con['F6 - F0']['mean_diff']:+.4f}; Holm p {Z3['F6-F0']['p_t_holm']:.3f}, publisher-level p {Z3['F6-F0']['publisher_level_p_t2']:.2f}); and F1 − F0 favoured the 12-month window at journal level (a difference of {con['F1 - F0']['mean_diff']:+.4f}, below 0.5% of loss) but not at imprint level. We therefore treat the ranking among journal-specific forecasters as suggestive: the differences are below 2% of loss. Additional forecasters tried during review (skew-normal, pooled-shape and blended; exploratory, reported in the repository's review notes rather than as archived scripts) and our learned forecaster L were no better than F0 (L − F0 {CM['F10_minus_F0']['mean']:+.4f}, 95% CI {CM['F10_minus_F0']['ci'][0]:+.4f} to {CM['F10_minus_F0']['ci'][1]:+.4f}). The lognormal forecasters also overstate the chance of acceptance within 72 days.")
