# ---------------------------------------------------------------- discussion
H("Discussion")
P(f"Three findings stand out. First, a journal's own recent history is informative but modestly so: it cuts forecast loss by about {pc(fm['F0']['skill'],0)} relative to ignoring journal identity within a publisher, yet {pc(vc['sigma2_resid']/tot,0)} of the variance lies between articles, so 80% intervals span roughly a five-fold range of days. The variance partition agrees with the earlier finding that most variance lies among articles within a journal ⟦bjork2013⟧ on a much larger and more recent dataset, and is not claimed as new. Second, simple forecasts are over-confident by about two to three points of coverage. Finite-sample error and a stationary process with growth and delayed visibility do not account for this, whereas random cohort-to-cohort shifts of the size of the journal × year variance component produce a shortfall of the same order; the shortfall is compatible with a variance-inflation effect (a forecast that ignores the variance of the next cohort's location is too narrow), but this is a consistency check rather than a causal finding: we cannot say why cohorts differ, the variance was estimated from the same data, and the simulation does not reproduce the spread across journals. Strictly out-of-time recalibration, width inflation and shrinkage all over-covered at the evaluation origins, and the early-origin shortfall from which the corrections were learned was larger, so these simple corrections were not dependable; whether a better-designed remedy exists is untested (see the learned forecaster, below). Third, the simplest estimator was hard to beat: lognormal, shrinkage and learned forecasters did not improve on empirical quantiles of the last 12 months, in line with the left-skewed, heavy-tailed shape of within-journal log durations (median skewness {M['shape']['median_skew']:.2f} across {M['shape']['n_journals']} journals); but only the value of journal information passed both Holm adjustment and a publisher-level check, and the shortfall was negative in all three publishers, so the ranking among journal-specific forecasters should be regarded as suggestive.")
P("For authors, evaluators and publishers the practical message is about reporting. Medians alone hide a wide and drifting distribution; statements of the form 'X% of papers are accepted within T days' should name the cohort (accepted or submitted), the period, whether desk rejections and article types with fast lanes are included, and should give quantiles with uncertainty. Phillips and Horn found inconsistent reporting among health-policy journals ⟦phillips2025⟧, and our results quantify how much a single past statistic can mislead: forecast probabilities of acceptance within 72 days were too extreme (reliability slope below one), so past shares overstate how reliably a fast journal stays fast. These are statistics about published, accepted papers only, and they should not be read as guarantees or as probabilities that a submission is accepted.")
P("The study complements descriptive analyses of publication delay and the planned PubMed-wide model ⟦dianovics_lifecycle,himmelstein,petrou⟧ by testing forecasts out of time, across three publishers and the fields they cover, and by showing why single past statistics should be reported as quantiles for explicit cohorts with uncertainty.")
H("Limitations")
for t in ["Conditioning on acceptance: only accepted papers have dates; rejected and withdrawn submissions, which may differ in duration, are unobserved, so results do not describe the time or chance of a given submission being accepted.",
          "Date semantics: the received date is publisher-defined. It is validated only for Springer Nature journals through Research Square preprints (a self-selected subset); Wiley and Taylor & Francis dates, and the finding that a minority of journals accept most papers within 72 days, rest on unverified definitions. Crossref–PubMed agreement does not verify them. Attempts to compare with publisher article pages were blocked.",
          f"Frame and generalisation: three publishers, journals with about 150–3,000 dated articles per year, 56% Springer Nature among evaluated journals; Elsevier, IEEE and others are outside the frame; the external check ({Y0['E9_eligible_journals']} journals, non-random) is inconclusive. The frame was defined from 2023 volumes, which may favour journals active in 2023; an eligibility rule using only pre-origin data gave a similar shortfall.",
          "Inference and design: two confirmatory origins (ten in an exploratory panel), overlapping information sets, and only three publishers as the highest-level clusters, so journal-cluster intervals are optimistic and imprint-level t(6) intervals are approximate; at publisher level only the value of journal information passed Holm adjustment, and the shortfall was negative in all three publishers (unadjusted p = 0.024 with 2 degrees of freedom). Several tests are post hoc or exploratory. The explanation of the shortfall is characterised rather than identified: the cohort-shift simulation depends on a variance component estimated from the same data, the stationary simulation assumes the process it tests, and neither reproduces the observed spread across journals or imprints.",
          "Learned forecaster: L was trained on one cohort (two for the 2025 origin), without tuning, so its failure to beat F0 is weak evidence about learned forecasters in general. Article types and batches: durations mix article types and fast lanes; our variants reduce but do not remove this, and article type is not observed.",
          "Remedy: under the strict out-of-time rule recalibration and width inflation were evaluated at three origins and over-corrected; the first-pass look-ahead variant is not evidence for them. The posting-lag validation of the received date may itself be shifted by up to 14 days and by calendar time.",
          "Prior work and novelty: the literature search was not systematic and most full texts were not read; the claim of novelty is therefore stated as 'we found no'. No human reviewer has examined the work.",
          "Scope: the study analyses statistics about editorial processes, not peer review itself."]:
    p = doc.add_paragraph(cite_sub(t), style="List Bullet"); p.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(3)
H("Conclusions")
P("A journal's acceptance times over the last year are a useful but wide, slightly over-confident forecast for its next papers. Simple empirical quantiles were as accurate as more elaborate forecasters, simple recalibration, width inflation and shrinkage learned from earlier cohorts over-covered, and 'within 72 days' claims were supported for a minority of journal-origins, with forecast probabilities that were too extreme. Turnaround statistics should be reported as drifting distributions with explicit cohorts and uncertainty.")
H("Abbreviations")
