          "Question. Statistics about how long journals take to accept papers (medians, “accepted within X days”) are used by authors, funders and evaluators, but are rarely checked and have not been tested as forecasts. We ask how well a journal's visible past predicts its next papers and how far “within X days” claims can be trusted.", "",
          f"Evidence. Using publisher-deposited dates for {M['n_records']:,} articles in {M['n_journals']} journals, we scored forecasts built only from information visible at the time against {M['n_target_papers']:,} later submissions. Journal history reduces forecast loss by {pc(fm['F0']['skill'],0)} relative to publisher-pooled forecasts, but intervals are wide and over-confident by two to three coverage points; the shortfall vanishes under random splitting and in a stationary simulation but is of the order produced by random cohort-to-cohort shifts of the size of the estimated journal × year variance; simple recalibration learned strictly from earlier cohorts over-corrects; and only {pc(g[(72,0.8)]['share'])} of journal-origins accepted at least 80% of papers within 72 days. We tested the validity of the received date against preprint posting dates for Springer Nature journals and could not do so for the others. Most pre-specified hypotheses were not supported or were inconclusive and we report this. The work was done and reviewed entirely by AI instances without human review, which we disclose.", "",
